== 8. Temptations

#strong[The eighth #emph[device] that Satan has to keep souls in a doubting and questioning condition is,]

BY persuading them that their state is not good, their hearts are not upright and their graces are not sound, because they are so followed, vexed and tormented with temptations. It is his method first to vex and weary the soul with temptations, and then to tempt the soul to think that surely it is not beloved,#footnote[He may tempt so as to make a saint weary of his life. Job 10:1: “My soul is weary of my life.”] because it is so much tempted. By this stratagem he keeps many precious souls in a sad, doubting and mourning state for many years, as many of the precious sons of Zion have found by woeful experience, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is solemnly to consider that those who have been best and most beloved have been most tempted by Satan. Though Satan can never rob a Christian of his crown, yet such is his malice that he will tempt them for this very reason, that he may rob them of their comforts. Such is his enmity to the Father that the nearer and dearer any child is to him, the more Satan will trouble him and vex him with temptations.#footnote[Pirates do not usually attack poor, empty vessels, and beggars need not fear the thief. Those who have most of God and are richest in grace will be most attacked by Satan, who is the greatest and wisest pirate in the world.] Christ himself was nearest and dearest, most innocent and most excellent, and yet none was so much tempted as Christ. David was dearly beloved, and yet was tempted by Satan to number the people. Job was highly praised by God himself, and yet much tempted; witness those sad things that fell from his mouth when he was wet to the skin. Peter was much prized by Christ; witness that choice testimony Christ gave of his faith and happiness, his showing him his glory on the mount, and that eye of pity he cast on him after his fearful fall, etc. And yet he was tempted by Satan. And the Lord said,#footnote[Luke 22:31, 32.] “Simon, Simon, Satan has asked to sift each of you#super[+] like wheat. But I have prayed for you, that your faith will not fail,” etc.

Paul had the honor of being exalted as high as heaven and of seeing glory that could not be expressed; and yet no sooner had he stepped out of heaven than he was buffeted by Satan, #emph[so that he would not be exalted above measure.] If#footnote[2 Cor 12:2, 7.] these who were so really, so gloriously, so eminently beloved by God, if these who have lived in heaven and set their feet on the stars, have been tempted, let no saints judge themselves not to be beloved because they are tempted.

It is as natural for saints who are dearly beloved to be tempted as it is for the sun to shine or a bird to sing. The eagle does not complain of her wings, nor the peacock of his train, nor the nightingale of her voice, because these are natural to them; no more should saints complain of their temptations, because they are natural to them. “For our struggle is not against#footnote[Eph 6:12.] flesh and blood, but against the rulers, against the authorities, against the powers of this world’s darkness, and against the spiritual forces of evil in the heavenly realms.”

The second #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider that all the temptations that befall the saints will be sanctified to them by a hand of love. Ah! the choice experiences the saints gain of the power of God supporting them, of the wisdom of God directing them (so to handle their spiritual weapons, their graces, as not only to resist but to overcome), and of the mercy and goodness of the Lord pardoning and helping them! And therefore Paul says, “I was given … a messenger of#footnote[2 Cor 12:7. #emph[Vide Bezam, Grotium, & Estium] \[See Beza, Grotius and Estius\]. Luther said there were three things that made a preacher: meditation, prayer and temptation.] Satan, to buffet me, so that I would not be exalted, so that I would not be exalted above measure.” It is there twice in that verse: he begins with it and ends with it. If he had not been buffeted, who knows how his heart would have swelled? He might have been carried higher in conceit than he had been before in his ecstasy. The school of temptation is a choice school: a school in which God gives his people the clearest and sweetest revelations of his love; a school in which God teaches his people to be more frequent and fervent in duty (when Paul was buffeted, then he prayed three times, that is, frequently and fervently); a school in which God teaches his people to be more tender, meek and compassionate to other poor tempted souls than ever; a school in which God teaches his people to see a greater evil in sin than ever, a greater emptiness in the creature than ever, and a greater need of Christ and free grace than ever; a school in which God will teach his people that all temptations are only his goldsmiths, by which he will try and refine his people and make them more bright and glorious. The outcome of all temptations will be the good of the saints, as you may see by the temptations that Adam and Eve, and Christ, and David, and Job, and Peter, and Paul met with. Those hands of power and love that bring light out of darkness, good out of evil, sweet out of bitter, life out of death, heaven out of hell, will bring much sweetness and good to his people out of all the temptations that come on them.

The third #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is wisely to consider that no temptations hurt or harm the saints so long as the saints resist them and the temptations prove the greatest affliction that can befall them. It is not Satan’s tempting but your#super[+] assenting, not his enticing but your yielding, that makes temptations hurtful to your souls. If the soul, when it is tempted, resists the temptation and says with Christ, “Get behind Me, Satan!” and with that young convert, “I am not the man I#footnote[Ego non sum ego.] was,” or, as Luther counsels all men to answer all temptations, with these words (#emph[Christianus sum]), “I am a Christian”; and if a man’s temptations are his greatest affliction, then the temptation is no sin on his soul, though it is a trouble on his mind. Suppose a soul can look the Lord in the face and say, “Ah Lord! I have many outward troubles on me now. I have lost such and such a near mercy, and such and such dear, desirable mercies. And yet you who know the heart, you know that all my crosses and losses do not make so many wounds in my soul, nor fetch so many sighs from my heart or tears from my eyes, as those temptations do with which Satan pursues my soul.” When it is like this with the soul, then temptations are only the soul’s trouble; they are not the soul’s sin.

Satan is a malicious and envious enemy;#footnote[Sometimes he shows his malice by leaving with the soul such things as may most vex and plague it, as Gregory observes in his leaving Job’s wife. This was not out of forgetfulness or carelessness, or any love or pity for Job, but to vex and torment him, etc., and to work on him to blaspheme God, despair and die, etc.] as his names are, so is he. His names are all names of enmity: the #emph[Accuser,] the #emph[Tempter,] the #emph[Destroyer,] the #emph[Devourer,] the #emph[envious Man.] This malice and envy of his he sometimes shows by tempting men to sins quite contrary to the temperament of their bodies, as he tempted Vespasian and Julian, men of sweet and excellent natures, to be most bloody murderers. Sometimes he shows his malice by tempting men to things that will bring him no honor or profit, etc. (“Fall down and worship me”): to blasphemy and atheism, etc., the very thoughts and first motions of which make the heart and the flesh tremble. And sometimes he shows his malice by tempting them to sins that they have not found their natures prone to, and that they abhor in others, etc.

Now if the soul resists these, complains of these, groans and mourns under these, and looks up to the Lord Jesus to be delivered from these, then they will not be put down to the soul’s account but to Satan’s; and he will be tormented all the more, the more maliciously the saints have been tempted by him, etc.

Make immediate and peremptory resistance against Satan’s temptations; bid defiance to the temptation at first sight.#footnote[When the emperor Constantine was told that there was no means to cure his leprosy except bathing his body in the blood of infants, he answered at once (#emph[malo semper aegrotare quam tali remedio convalescere]), “I would rather not be cured than use such a remedy.”] It is safe to resist; it is dangerous to dispute. Eve lost herself and her posterity by entering the lists of dispute, when she should have resisted and stood on terms of defiance with Satan. He who would stand in the hour of temptation must plead with Christ, “It is written”; he who would triumph over temptations must always plead, “It is written.” Satan is bold and impudent, and if you#super[+] are not peremptory in your resistance, he will make fresh attacks on you. It is your#super[+] greatest honor and your highest wisdom to withstand the beginnings of a temptation peremptorily, for a remedy afterward often comes too late.

Mistress Katherine Brettergh, once, after a great conflict with Satan, said, “Do not reason with me; I am only a weak woman. If you have anything to say, say it to my Christ. He is my advocate, my strength and my redeemer, and he will plead for me.”

Men must not seek to resist Satan’s craft with craft (#emph[sed per apertum martem]) but by open defiance. He who thinks to put Satan off by disputing and reasoning shoots with Satan in his own bow. As soon as a temptation shows its face, say to the temptation, as Ephraim said to his idols, “Get away from me! What more have I#footnote[Hos 14.] to do with you?” Oh! say to the temptation, as David said to the sons of Zeruiah, “What have I to do with you#super[+]?”#footnote[2 Sam 16:10.] You will be too strong for me. He who resists temptations in this way will never be undone by temptation, etc.

Make strong and constant resistance#footnote[I have read of one who, being tempted with offers of money to desert Christ, gave this excellent answer: “Let no man think that he who has forsaken his own goods to follow Christ will take other men’s goods to forsake Christ.”] against Satan’s temptations. Resist temptations with arguments drawn from the honor of God, the love of God, and your union and communion with God; from the blood of Christ, the death of Christ, the kindness of Christ, the intercession of Christ and the glory of Christ; from the voice of the Spirit, the counsel of the Spirit, the comforts of the Spirit, the presence of the Spirit, the seal of the Spirit, the whisperings of the Spirit, the commands of the Spirit, the assistance of the Spirit, the witness of the Spirit; and from the glory of heaven, the excellence of grace, the beauty of holiness, the worth of the soul, and the vileness, bitterness and evil of sin, the least sin being a greater evil than the greatest temptation in the world.

And see that you#super[+] make constant resistance as well as strong resistance. Be constantly in arms; Satan will come on with new temptations when old#footnote[Luke 4:13: “When the devil had finished every temptation, he left Him until an opportune time.” Christ had no rest until he had been thoroughly tried with all kinds of temptations. Calvin.] ones are too weak. In a calm, prepare for a storm. The tempter is restless, impudent and subtle. He will suit his temptations to your#super[+] constitutions and inclinations; Satan loves to sail with the wind. If your#super[+] knowledge is weak, he will tempt you to error; if your consciences are tender, he will tempt you to scrupulosity and too much strictness, such as doing nothing but hearing, praying, reading, etc. If your#super[+] consciences are wide and lax, he will tempt you to carnal security; if you are bold-spirited, he will tempt you to presumption; if timid, to despair; if flexible, to inconstancy; if proud and stiff, to gross folly. Therefore always be ready for fresh assaults; make one victory a step to another. When you#super[+] have overcome a temptation, beware of unbending your bow, and see to it that your bow is always bent and keeps its strength. When you#super[+] have overcome one temptation, you must be ready to enter the lists with another. As distrust (in some sense) is the mother of safety, so security is the gate of danger. A man needs to fear this most of all, that he does not fear all. If Satan is always roaring, we should always be watching and resisting him. And certainly, he who makes strong and constant resistance to Satan’s temptations will in the end get above his temptations, and for the present is safe enough from being ruined by his temptations, etc.

For a close of this, remember that it is#footnote[He who will yield to sin to be rid of temptation will be tempted all the more, and be less able to withstand temptations.] dangerous to yield to the least sin to be rid of the greatest temptation. To take this course would be as if a man thought to wash himself clean in ink, or as if a man exchanged a light cross made of paper for an iron cross that is heavy, toilsome and bloody. The least sin set home on the conscience will wound, vex and oppress the soul more than all the temptations in the world can. Therefore never yield to the least sin to be rid of the greatest temptation. Sidonius Apollinaris relates how a certain man named Maximus, who had reached the top of honor by crooked means, was very much wearied on the first day, and fetching a deep sigh, said, “O Damocles! how happy I count you for having been a king only for the length of a dinner. I have been one a whole day, and can bear it no longer.” I will leave you#super[+] to make the application.
